## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="What belongs in a prompt version?" answer="The instruction, relevant policy text, model settings, and references required to reproduce behavior." /%}

{% faq-item question="Can copy edits skip review?" answer="Not when they can alter task interpretation, tool use, or a policy boundary." /%}

{% faq-item question="What triggers rollback?" answer="A predeclared unacceptable behavior or a material regression in reviewed outcomes." /%}

## A practical version record

Keep the prompt and its operational dependencies together so a run can be reproduced and a release can be rolled back.

```yaml
prompt_id: support-triage
version: 2026-08-26.4
model: approved-model-id
policy: support-policy-12
evaluation_set: support-triage-regression
rollback_when: unacceptable_policy_failure
```

> **Decision rule:** A prompt version is incomplete if an operator cannot identify the model, policy, evaluation set, and rollback condition that accompanied it.

## Next step: make prompt versioning actionable

Start with one workflow this week. Instrument the evidence needed to explain a single completed run, review it against the intended outcome, and turn the finding into a named test or operating decision. Then use the relevant Uncoil resource above to inspect the connected execution path. The final CTA is deliberately practical: choose one bounded improvement, measure its effect, and keep the rollback path clear.



## Put Prompt Versioning and Prompt Management into practice

### A small release checklist

- Define the user-visible outcome and the boundary this article does not cover.
- Capture the evidence needed to explain movement, including the relevant version, cohort, and failure class.
- Assign one owner for the next bounded action and write the stop condition before changing production behavior.

> **Decision rule:** Prefer the smallest change that produces inspectable evidence. If the example cannot be tested, label it illustrative and verify the surrounding assumptions before adapting it.

